  "replies": [
   "I've built an nf-core/atacseq run for the 4 K562 Omni-ATAC samples. It isn't submitted yet.\n\n**What you'll get:** the open-chromatin peaks for each sample and for each group. The pipeline also counts reads at a shared set of peaks and runs DESeq2 on those counts, comparing JQ1-treated (500 nM) with DMSO. That comparison shows where chromatin opens or closes with JQ1. With only 2 replicates per group the statistics will be weak, so treat the differential results as a first look. Three or more replicates per group would make them much more reliable.\n\n**Samplesheet:** one sheet, 4 paired-end samples.\n- DMSO: D.SEQ-261110NA-1 (replicate 1), D.SEQ-261110NA-2 (replicate 2)\n- JQ1: D.SEQ-261110NA-3 (replicate 1), D.SEQ-261110NA-4 (replicate 2)\n\n**Run settings:**\n- **Reference:** the local Luria human GRCh38 reference (GRCh38.primary_assembly.genome.fa.gz with gencode.v46.basic.annotation.gtf.gz).\n- **Aligner:** bwa.\n- **Peaks:** broad peaks, which is the default; I can switch to narrow peaks.\n- **No input controls.**\n- **Read length:** set to 50. The reads are actually 38 bp, but 50 is the smallest value the pipeline accepts. It's only used to estimate genome size for peak calling.\n- **Saved index:** the genome index is kept for later runs.\n\n**Run time:** there's no estimate for this pipeline yet.\n\nThe run is built and ready. Let me know if you want to change anything, such as narrow peaks or a different aligner. Once it finishes, just tell Nessie \"my run is done\" and Nessie will turn the outputs into NExtSEEK upload workbooks for you to review."
  ],
